CP(1)                          P32W Unix                           CP(1)

NAME
       cp - copy files

SYNOPSIS
       cp [-r] from to
       cp [-r] file ... directory

DESCRIPTION
       In the first form cp copies the file from to the file to. In
       the second, when the last argument is a directory, each file
       is copied into it under its last path component (cp a/x d
       makes d/x).

       A new target is created with the permission bits (the low
       nine) of the source, less the umask; an existing target is
       truncated and overwritten and keeps its own mode and owner.
       The copy is made 1024 bytes at a time and is exact, so cp
       serves for text and binary files alike.

       cp refuses to copy a file onto itself, by whatever path it
       is named (cp x ./x, cp x .): "cp: x and ./x are the same
       file". Without -r a directory given as a source is refused
       ("cp: d: is a directory").

       If a file cannot be copied cp says why and goes on with the
       next.

OPTIONS
       -r, -R copy directories recursively: each directory is made
              in the target (with the source's mode, plus owner
              rwx) or, if it exists, copied into, and everything
              in it is copied below it. A tree is not copied into
              itself: cp -r d d/sub copies d without the new
              d/sub.
       --     ends the options.

EXAMPLES
       cp /etc/motd motd.old         copy a file
       cp *.c /usr/ken/src           copy several into a directory
       cp -r src /mnt/backup         copy a tree

EXIT STATUS
       0 if every file was copied; 1 if any could not be (not found,
       the same file, a directory without -r, a read or write
       error), or if there are more than two arguments and the last
       is not a directory; 2 for a usage error (fewer than two
       arguments, an unknown option).

SEE ALSO
       mv(1), ln(1), cat(1), tar(1)

BUGS
       There are no -i, -f or -p options. Times, owner and set-id
       bits are not copied. Devices met by -r are read like files,
       and every name of a file with several links becomes a
       separate copy.
